BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Antarctica: The Frozen Desert | Photographs by Vegar Abelsnes
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T080000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T170000
UID:260914kT41rgRg3UiKEiAoXOzQ5w
DESCRIPTION:1st Street Gallery at WTRSHD will present *“Antarctica: The 
 Frozen Desert\,”* a new body of fine art photography by Vegar Abelsnes\,
  opening September 26th\, 2026\, and on view September through November. 
  The exhibition marks a deliberate turn in Abelsnes’ photography\; after
  more than two decades of working in a commercial and advertising capacity
 \, he has embarked on a career as a photo and field guide in the polar reg
 ions. The exhibit features work generated over 6 *voyages to the* Antarcti
 c Peninsula. He is returning to Antarctica in January and will add the Fal
 klands and South Georgia to his list of destinations to guide and photogra
 ph.\n\nTraveling to some of the most remote and inhospitable coastlines on
  Earth\, Abelsnes has photographed a landscape that exists almost entirely
  outside of human time. The ice in these images began forming millions of 
 years ago\, while Homo sapiens have only existed for roughly 300\,000 year
 s. Defined by such a scale\, Antarctica is less a place than a record — 
 vast\, indifferent\, treacherous\, and staggeringly beautiful\, holding a 
 kind of cosmic truth that Abelsnes says is more important now than ever be
 fore.
LOCATION:1st Street Gallery @ WTRSHD
URL:https://vegarabelsnes.com/ANTARCTICA:-The-Frozen-Desert/thumbs
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:An Online Show and Tell / Q & A with Cory Infinite x Svea Herstar
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T140000
UID:261024gbL0tOTR4E-smXQYmxq2dw
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, November 7\, 2026 12:00 pm to 2:00 pm\n\nNoon (PST)
  / 2 PM Kansas City / 3 PM EST\n\nLocation\n\nOnline – Zoom A Zoom link 
 will be sent out 24 hours before the event date to the registrants.\n\nCat
 egory\n\nEvents\, Other\, Sustainability\n\nExperience Level\n\nAll Levels
 \n\nAges\n\nAll Ages\n\nRegistration Cost per Viewer:\n\n$10\n\nJoin Cory 
 Infinite & Svea Herstar on Zoom for a live upcycled wearable art demo and 
 Q&A with Trashion Fashion Sonoma and SCC.\n\n**program overview**\n\nIf yo
 u’ve been on Instagram lately and your algorithm recognizes you are of 
 the niche community of people that like to create\, turn trash into treasu
 re or love an upcycled wearable art project then it’s no doubt Cory Infi
 nite (Trashion Fashion Sonoma Runway guest judge) has turned up on your In
 stagram feed! Trashion Fashion Sonoma & Sonoma Community Center team up wi
 th Kansas City\, MO designers Cory Infinite & Svea Herstar to hear about 
 how they create\, their process and some of the tools they have used on th
 eir famous fits such as the Scrabble Hoodie ( a together project\,) the Ke
 yboard jumpsuit (Cory) or the Trash Trench (Svea.) After demo\, they will
  answer as many questions as time allows. Check out bios below! As Trashio
 n Fashion Sonoma rolls out their 17th annual Trashion Runway applications 
 in January 2027 – now is the time to get inspired\, look at alternative
  ways to construct and deconstruct materials to tell the story of wearable
  art.  **Donations are accepted of any amount and supports the artists\, 
 Cory and Svea and continued Sustainability/ Trashion programming and event
 s for non profit\, Sonoma Community Center.**
LOCATION:Sonoma Community Center
URL:https://sonomacommunitycenter.org/programs/an-online-show-and-tell-q-a
 -with-cory-infinite-x-svea-herstar/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Burgeon An exhibit by Ceramics Resident Artist Gabriel John Pouche
 r
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T190000
UID:261024CVVM4ftC2UOaCizmIGhtZw
DESCRIPTION:On View\n\nNovember 7 – 29\, 2026\n\nOpening Reception\n\nSa
 t\, November 7 · 5 – 7 PM\n\nwhere\n\nGallery 212 276 E. Napa St.\, Son
 oma\n\nadmission\n\nFree Open to the public\n\nArtist Statement\n\nGabriel
 ’s work stems from the intersection of ecology and queer identity\, and 
 the parallel frameworks they provide for resisting rigid categories\, bina
 ry distinctions\, and expected outcomes. Burgeon examines the notion that 
 transformation does not require resolution. The work draws inspiration fro
 m natural processes of growth and consumption\, and the way they blur the 
 boundaries between organism and environment\, individual and collective. C
 onstructing clay forms through an extended process of repetition and accum
 ulation\, pattern and deviation\, Gabriel’s work explores the idea that 
 to be in flux is not to be incomplete.
LOCATION:Sonoma Community Center
URL:https://sonomacommunitycenter.org/programs/burgeon-exhibit/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
