BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:A Taste of Italy at Centennial Mountain Tasting Room
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T170000
UID:260430uBlEk8W79E2W_Q9CtTKL8A
DESCRIPTION:For wine drinkers eager to experience world-class wines made i
 n California from Italian varieties\, the Centennial Mountain Tasting Room
  is a must during any trip to Sonoma County. Located just off the plaza in
  the charming town of Healdsburg near famed restaurants\, boutiques and ga
 lleries\, the Centennial Mountain Tasting Room is a destination that invit
 es discovery. Founded by the acclaimed team that guides renowned Pinot Noi
 r and Chardonnay producer Rhys Vineyards\, Centennial Mountain was establi
 shed to explore Italian grape varieties grown on its acclaimed Centennial 
 Mountain Vineyard in the rugged mountains of Northern Sonoma County.\n\nTh
 e Centennial Mountain Vineyard Tasting features the winery’s coveted Neb
 biolo\, and the pioneering Carricante and Nerello Mascalese wines\, among 
 others. To showcase how beautifully the wines pair with food\, the winery 
 also offers a delicious Wine and Sushi Pairing from nearby Asahi Sushi\, o
 ne of Healdsburg’s most popular dining destinations.\n\nGuests can also 
 enhance their tasting experience with a curated salumi board from Journeym
 an Meat Company. This generous accompaniment features six house-made salum
 i\, crostini\, cornichons\, olives\, and a selection of local cheese that 
 serves 2 to 5 guests.
LOCATION:Centennial Mountain Tasting Room
URL:https://cmvineyard.com/visit/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Beginner Salsa Friday
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T193000
UID:260317ggSfSFL8VEyCvDUyReKBLw
DESCRIPTION:Join our beginner salsa class. No experience or partner is nee
 ded. Just comfortable shoes and desire to have fun!\n\nGreat for first tim
 ers!\n\nNo dance experience required\n\nNon-marking shoes soles\n\nNo part
 ner necessary\n\nWe know it can be challenging to learn something new. As 
 such\, the beginner salsa class is geared for people who are starting out 
 or those that are returning to refresh their skills. Our relaxed environme
 nt is perfect for learning salsa basics\, as we make it fun and enjoyable.
  In this class you will learn basic directional steps\, proper frame for p
 artner-work and simple turn patterns. We organize our curriculum so that t
 he movements are easy to remember. Join us to learn the fundamentals! No e
 xperience is necessary and no partner is required. Drop-in fee is $20.\n\n
 Date & Time: Fridays @ 6:30PM – 7:30PM\n\nAddress: 4140 Redwood Highway 
 #8 San Rafael\, CA\n\nNote: Arrive 10 minutes before. No floor marking sho
 e soles allowed.
LOCATION:Salsa Dance Marin
URL:https://salsadancemarin.com/615pm-beginner-salsa-group-lesson/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Antarctica: The Frozen Desert | Photographs by Vegar Abelsnes
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T080000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261120T170000
UID:2609143gYHn40640mzmquHDJwKJw
DESCRIPTION:1st Street Gallery at WTRSHD will present *“Antarctica: The 
 Frozen Desert\,”* a new body of fine art photography by Vegar Abelsnes\,
  opening September 26th\, 2026\, and on view September through November. 
  The exhibition marks a deliberate turn in Abelsnes’ photography\; after
  more than two decades of working in a commercial and advertising capacity
 \, he has embarked on a career as a photo and field guide in the polar reg
 ions. The exhibit features work generated over 6 *voyages to the* Antarcti
 c Peninsula. He is returning to Antarctica in January and will add the Fal
 klands and South Georgia to his list of destinations to guide and photogra
 ph.\n\nTraveling to some of the most remote and inhospitable coastlines on
  Earth\, Abelsnes has photographed a landscape that exists almost entirely
  outside of human time. The ice in these images began forming millions of 
 years ago\, while Homo sapiens have only existed for roughly 300\,000 year
 s. Defined by such a scale\, Antarctica is less a place than a record — 
 vast\, indifferent\, treacherous\, and staggeringly beautiful\, holding a 
 kind of cosmic truth that Abelsnes says is more important now than ever be
 fore.
LOCATION:1st Street Gallery @ WTRSHD
URL:https://vegarabelsnes.com/ANTARCTICA:-The-Frozen-Desert/thumbs
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
